 // is aperture independent.
+// Axial chromatic aberration reduced to the one number the shaders need: the radius of the
+// SHRUNK channels' disc, in units of the full one. It is a ratio between wavelengths, a property
+// of the glass, so it is deliberately NOT aperture scaled - the discs themselves already shrink
+// with the circle of confusion, which is why stopping down hides the fringing on its own.
+// See AxialChromaticSpec.md.
+static float ResolveAxialChromaticScale(float amount)
+{
+	const float kAxial = 0.35f;   // how far the shrunk channels fall behind at amount = 1
+	return 1.0f - kAxial * clamp_tpl(amount, 0.0f, 1.0f);
